 "findings": [
  "Cycle 1.28 s (catalogue 128 ms; frames 1 to 11 = 10 intervals = one full cycle of two steps, one riser per step). Our human.climb is 8 frames at 8 fps = 1.0 s: about 22% faster than this unhurried stair climb.",
  "Duty: foot A is down in frames 1-7 of the cycle frames 1-10 (lifts between 7 and 8) = 0.7; foot B is down in frames 1-2 (3 uncertain) and 7-10 = 0.6-0.7. Duty about 0.65 +- 0.05 (frame resolution is 0.1 cycle), with double support around frames 1-3 and 7. Our climb.duty 0.6 is at the low end; 0.65-0.7 would match stairs.",
  "Knee lift: the swing thigh rises to 52-55 deg forward of vertical (frames 5-6 for B, 9-10 for A) with the knee at 87-93 deg; just after lift-off the knee folds to 68 deg (frame 9). The leading leg lands with knee 96-110 deg and thigh about 50 deg forward, then straightens to 166-168 deg by mid-stance.",
  "Foot lift: the swing toe rises about 100 px = 0.45 leg lengths (leg about 220 px) from the step it left, i.e. two risers (riser about 45 px = 0.2 leg lengths), and peaks only about 14 px = 0.06 leg lengths above the step it lands on. Our climb.lift 4 units = 0.11 of our 37-unit leg is a pull-off from a wall, not a stair clearance, so these are not directly comparable; for stairs, a clearance of about 0.06 leg lengths above the next tread is what the plate shows.",
  "Trunk lean: 11-27 deg forward (mean about 21 deg, increasing as the climber looks down at the steps); head bowed. Our climb.lean -6 pitches the body back toward a vertical wall; a stair climb leans forward instead.",
